the summer is here the time of year when  so many people's thoughts turn to taking  a holiday  except this year will be very different  across some of our  favorite european destinations the rules  are tightening  portugal the latest country to impose a  quarantine on the british visitors who  are allowed in  that's now the default position for most  european nations  the situation that applies to the united  kingdom at present is that um travelers  who are vaccinated  who are fully vaccinated uh or who have  an essential reason  to come to the eu should be allowed by  member states uh  to um to arrive into the eu and um  i can i can also add that from the  commission side  we of course expect member states to  apply this recommendation fully there is  a clear message from germany and france  that they expect european countries to  put health concerns above  tourist revenue and to either deny entry  to visitors from the uk  or to impose a lengthy quarantine for  those whose businesses  depend on british tourists this is a  bleak time  the algarve should be alive with people  instead it's quiet  the algarve was british tourism  it is british tourism without british  tourism without those numbers  then it becomes very difficult to keep  hotels open  um it becomes very difficult to keep  restaurants open bars open  and when they can't stay open then  obviously the area closes  and then even the smaller percentages of  people like the spanish the italians the  french  dutch that also come to the algarve but  in much smaller numbers  even them combined uh it's not enough to  replace the brits  but in brussels they insist it's too  early to make concessions  european diplomats i've spoken to say  they are very wary about the rising  number  of delta variant cases in the uk and  they point out that just about  anyone arriving in britain has to  quarantine even if they're  fully vaccinated why they ask should it  be any different  for people making the reverse journey  from britain  into the european union so should both  sides be  cutting a bit more slack particularly  for those who are fully vaccinated  people who in theory  present a very low risk one of the  the things we're trying to do worldwide  is increase people's  willingness to accept the vaccine and  part of that is showing people what the  world is like when you're fully  vaccinated  because if people don't see the vaccine  as a path to get their life back to  pursue the values that are important to  them like traveling like going on  vacation  they're not going to get vaccinated  tourism is a touchstone to normality  that's why we pine for it  but for the moment it remains out of  reach a  hope rather than a reality  adam parsons sky news brussels
 

